## The format a value has on a device: `Dtype[T]` names the format of `T`.
#
# Why this module exists: Dawn has no type arguments at a call site (spec 2.5),
# so the only way to say "the bf16 format" where a function is called is to
# pass a value. std/gpu's format markers were that value, and they cost a
# second type per format beside the host value type of the same name
# (`gpu.BF16` is a label, `narrow.BF16` is a number). Here the host value type
# is the format: `Dtype[T]` carries its name at run time and `T` at the type
# level, and a type that has a device format declares, in its own module, a
# constant spelled like the type (`I8` beside `I8`) holding its `Dtype`. One
# `use std/int/i8.{I8}` then brings in the type and its witness together, and
# `alloc(I8, n)` reads as it always did. The premise std/gpu's header gave for
# not reusing the host types, that doing so needs a value made just to be a
# label, no longer holds; docs/tileir-011-design.md 2.4 has the whole account.
#
# Why a module of its own: the integer modules and std/narrow both declare
# witnesses, and neither may depend on std/gpu (it uses narrow), so the format
# type sits below all of them. This module imports nothing.
#
# Only std can mint a `Dtype`: its representation is opaque and `dtype_named`
# is `pub(pkg)`, so a program cannot claim that its own type has a device
# format. A type opts in with `impl HasDtype`, written in its own module. U16,
# U32 and U64 have none on purpose: the tile dialect has no unsigned integer
# types (signedness is a property of the operation), so a `Tensor[U32]` should
# be a type error at `alloc` and not a wrong guess.
#
# The three builtin formats live here because Float, Int and Bool belong to no
# module. Their witnesses are `F64`, `I64` and `I1`: a constant must be
# SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE, so `Float` cannot be the name of its own witness.

## A device format for values of type `T`.
pub opaque type Dtype[T] = String

## The format called `name`. Only std makes formats.
pub(pkg) fn dtype_named[T](name: String) -> Dtype[T] = name

## The name the format goes by in a tile dialect, such as `f64` or `bf16`.
pub fn dtype_name[T](d: Dtype[T]) -> String = d

## A type with a device format. `T` is only in the return position, so the
## expected type picks the impl in any other module: `let f: Dtype[Float] =
## dtype_of()`. (Inside this module an opaque type is its target, a `String`,
## so `T` is unconstrained and nothing here can infer it;
## scripts/spike-native/std_dtype.dawn covers the inference.)
pub trait HasDtype[T] {
  fn dtype_of() -> Dtype[T]
}

## The format of `Float`, 64-bit IEEE.
pub const F64: Dtype[Float] = dtype_named("f64")

## The format of `Int`, 64-bit two's complement.
pub const I64: Dtype[Int] = dtype_named("i64")

## The format of `Bool` in a tile: a one-bit mask.
pub const I1: Dtype[Bool] = dtype_named("i1")

impl HasDtype[Float] {
  fn dtype_of() -> Dtype[Float] = F64
}

impl HasDtype[Int] {
  fn dtype_of() -> Dtype[Int] = I64
}

impl HasDtype[Bool] {
  fn dtype_of() -> Dtype[Bool] = I1
}

test "the builtin witnesses carry the dialect's names" {
  assert dtype_name(F64) == "f64"
  assert dtype_name(I64) == "i64"
  assert dtype_name(I1) == "i1"
}

test "a format is as long-lived as its name" {
  let again: Dtype[Float] = dtype_named("f64")
  assert dtype_name(again) == dtype_name(F64)
  assert dtype_name(F64) != dtype_name(I64)
}
